  "query_preview": "..Moisture from Polo and the remnants of Odalys\ncontinue to overspread the area, with stratiform rain and \nembedded showers spreading across northern AZ as the mid/upper-\nlevel trough near California continues to moves into the lower \nColorado River Valley, with an closed upper-level low developing \nat the base of the trough, then slowly moving across the region \nTuesday. The risk for embedded thunderstorms will increase later \ntoday and into Tuesday due to limited daytime heating, along with \nimproved upper-level synoptic support associated with the trough \nand closed low. Some of these thunderstorms could become strong to\nsevere at times with heavy rain, strong winds, and large hail. \nWPC continues to message a moderate risk for excessive rainfall \nfor most of the region for today and then shifting focus for the \neastern part of the state Tuesday as the trough and closed upper- \nlevel low move eastward. A Flood Watch remains in effect through \nTuesday evening. Widespread rainfall amounts of 1 to 2 inches \nforecast across the high deserts of northeast Arizona have an \naverage return interval of 5 to 10 years, making this at least an \nuncommon event there. Locally heavy rainfall from thunderstorms, \nalong with upsloping and other terrain-effects could also lead to \nlocalized rainfall of 2-3 inches, especially over the heavier \nterrain and eastern AZ. High temperatures across the region today \nand Tuesday will be quite cool due to the cloud cover and \nrainfall, 10 to 20 degrees below average. By Wednesday night, \nprecipitation chances start winding down in the far eastern parts \nof the state as the trough sweeps east and takes the moisture \nplume with it.\n\nContinuing note: While the tropical moisture is associated with\ntropical systems, Odalys has dissipated over the open ocean and \nthe circulation associated with Polo will not actually make it up\ninto Arizona. Polo is expected to make landfall in Baja \nCalifornia today, cross the Gulf of California and break apart to\nour south when it makes landfall again in mainland Mexico.\n\nBy Thursday onward dry air from the west and north will take over\nand limit the potential for precipitation through the rest of the\nweek. Some ridging will moving in towards the end of the week \nlook for a pleasant start to October as high temperatures recover \nto a few degrees above average.",
